"""
Exact diagonalization of the Ising fusion category chain, block diagonalized by
lattice momentum AND by the full Ising-category symmetry {U(1), U(psi), U(sigma)}.

Implements Eqs. (7)--(8), (12)--(15) and (18)--(24) of the supplied manuscript
(dated September 9, 2026), with kappa=+1 and periodic boundary conditions.
Dependencies: Python >=3.10, NumPy, SciPy; Matplotlib only for the plot.

Charge sectors
--------------
The symmetry algebra (22) is commutative. Its three one-dimensional irreps,
Eq. (24), label the charge sectors used everywhere below:

    "1"     : (U(1), U(psi), U(sigma)) = (1,  1, +sqrt2)
    "psi"   : (1,  1, -sqrt2)
    "sigma" : (1, -1,  0)

Every (momentum m, charge) block is built and diagonalized separately. Summed
over m their dimensions are (D+1)/4, (D+1)/4 and (D-1)/2, with D = dim H.

How the full symmetry is incorporated
-------------------------------------
1. U(psi) swaps I<->psi on every mu site, a permutation that commutes with T.
   Orbit representatives of the abelian group G = <T, U(psi)> = Z_L x Z_2 give
   the (m, U(psi)=+-1) blocks with the same machinery, and the same sparsity,
   as momentum alone. Since U(sigma)U(psi) = U(sigma), U(sigma) vanishes on
   U(psi) = -1, so that block IS the "sigma" block. Nothing else is needed.

2. On U(psi) = +1, S = U(sigma)/sqrt2 is a Hermitian involution (Eq. 22c:
   S^2 = (1+U(psi))/2 = 1). U(sigma) exchanges sigma and mu segments (Eq. 19),
   so it maps the layer with n_s sigma sites onto the layer with L-n_s sigma
   sites. Every H_flip term changes n_s by exactly +-1 and H_dw is diagonal.
   Split the (m,+) space into

       A : n_s < L/2,        B = S(A) : n_s > L/2,

   and, at n_s = L/2 (even L), put one member of each pair of complementary
   grade necklaces [g] != [gbar] in A and the other in B. The
   self-complementary necklaces [g] = [gbar] form a small remainder E.
   The vectors (|a> +- S|a>)/sqrt2 (a in A), together with the S eigenvectors
   inside each E necklace (tiny dense blocks), are orthonormal bases of the
   S = +-1 eigenspaces, and [H, S] = 0 with S^2 = 1 gives exactly

       H_{+-} = H_AA +- S_AB H_BA        (plus rows and columns for E+-).

   Nothing is diagonalized, projected or Gram-Schmidt orthogonalized. Because
   H moves one layer at a time, H_BA and S_AB are nonzero only at the self-dual
   layer n_s ~ L/2. The "1" and "psi" blocks are the half-space Hamiltonian H_AA
   plus a boundary term at the fold, each about half of the (m,+) dimension.

3. In the Z_2 labels of the domains, S_AB is a Walsh-Hadamard transform (Eq. 20):
   a state with n sigma domains has 2**(n-1) images. As a sparse matrix it would
   dominate the cost, and the explicit product S_AB H_BA is denser still (about
   13x the factored cost at L=18). Large blocks are therefore applied as
   x -> H_AA x +- S_AB (H_BA x) inside Lanczos, with S_AB done as one (n-1)-bit
   fast Walsh-Hadamard transform per grade necklace: O(n 2**n) work instead of
   O(4**n). The rare periodic necklaces and E use sparse transitions. Small blocks
   are formed explicitly. SectorBlock.to_csr() always returns the explicit matrix.

4. H is real, so the blocks at m and L-m are complex conjugates. By default only
   m = 0..L//2 are diagonalized and the rest are copied (--all-momenta disables).

Validation: `python ising_category_ed.py --L 8 --validate` (any L <= 10) checks every
block against full ED built from the explicit reference operators below:
isometry, Q^dag H Q, T = e^{-ik}, U(psi) and U(sigma) eigenvalues, the Walsh-
Hadamard coupling against its sparse form, and the union of all block spectra.

Performance (2-core, 7 GB machine, r=0, theta=pi/4): L=18 full charge-resolved
spectrum with nev=8 in under 2 minutes. L=20 (dimension 4.5e7): ~3 s of setup,
blocks of dimension 5.7e5 ("1", "psi") and 1.1e6 ("sigma"), 1-2 minutes per
momentum, the full spectrum with nev=6 in 16 minutes at 3 GB peak memory.
ARPACK makes many small BLAS calls: when running several jobs at once, set
OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 to avoid oversubscribed BLAS threads.

Conventions (unchanged from the momentum-only version)
------------------------------------------------------
x labels I=0, PSI=1, SIGMA=2. T is RIGHT translation, numpy.roll(state, +1).
k = 2*pi*m/L. For a representative |a> with orbit O_a under G the symmetric ket is

    |a;k,p> = |O_a|**(-1/2) sum_{b in O_a} chi(g_b)|b>,   chi(T^t U(psi)^f) = e^{ikt} p^f,

where g_b maps a to b. Thus T|a;k,p> = e^{-ik}|a;k,p> and U(psi)|a;k,p> = p|a;k,p>.
If the representative of a target state s is T^t U(psi)^f s, the reduced
matrix element carries e^{+ikt} p^f sqrt(|O_a|/|O_b|). This is the previous
e^{+iks} sqrt(R_a/R_b) with the psi flip included. Representatives are the orbit
members with the smallest integer code (grade bits first, then label bits), not
the lexicographically smallest tuple. This changes basis phases, never spectra.

A state is stored as two L-bit integers: g (bit i set iff x_i = SIGMA) and
l (bit i set iff x_i = PSI). Right translation is a left bit rotation.

CFT scaling and spontaneous degeneracies refer to finite-size extrapolation,
not to exact degeneracy of generic short rings.
"""

